1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,560
Mistype one digit of your card number, and

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:04,640
a checkout form can spot it before it

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:05,920
contacts your bank.

4
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:08,000
There's no list of real cards in your

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,080
browser. All it needs is a little

6
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,120
arithmetic.

7
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:13,600
Here's the trick behind that check.

8
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,600
A card number isn't random.

9
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,000
The first digits identify the card issuer.

10
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,840
The digits after that identify the

11
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,480
account. And the last digit is a check

12
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:24,800
digit, calculated from all the others.

13
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,760
Its only job is to catch mistakes.

14
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:30,240
The method is named after Hans Peter Luhn

15
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,720
of IBM. His patent for a device that

16
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:34,800
calculated and checked these digits was

17
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:37,440
granted in nineteen sixty, decades before

18
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:38,800
online shopping.

19
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,800
Grab a pen if you want to follow along.

20
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:42,710
Let's run it on a short made up number:

21
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:46,560
four five three nine, one four eight nine.

22
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:48,640
Many cards have sixteen digits.

23
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:50,640
American Express cards have fifteen.

24
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:52,560
The steps are the same either way.

25
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,040
Start at the right, with the check digit.

26
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:56,160
Leave it alone.

27
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:58,560
Then double every second digit as you move

28
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:01,520
left: the eight, the one, the three and

29
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:04,330
the four. Leave the others as they are.

30
00:01:24,330 --> 00:01:25,770
Now double them.

31
00:01:25,770 --> 00:01:27,130
Four becomes eight.

32
00:01:27,130 --> 00:01:28,650
Three becomes six.

33
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:30,250
One becomes two.

34
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:32,250
Eight becomes sixteen, and that's two

35
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:34,650
digits. So add them: one plus six is

36
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:36,650
seven. That's the only extra rule.

37
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:38,810
If doubling gives a two digit number, add

38
00:01:38,970 --> 00:01:41,130
its digits together, or just subtract

39
00:01:41,130 --> 00:01:43,930
nine. Sixteen minus nine is also seven.

40
00:01:44,170 --> 00:01:46,250
Now add everything up.

41
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:49,610
Eight, five, six, nine,

42
00:01:49,610 --> 00:01:52,970
two, four, seven, nine.

43
00:01:52,970 --> 00:01:54,250
That's fifty.

44
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:55,450
And here's the rule.

45
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:57,690
If the total ends in zero, the number

46
00:01:57,690 --> 00:02:00,330
passes. Fifty ends in zero, so this

47
00:02:00,330 --> 00:02:02,410
example passes the Luhn check.

48
00:02:02,410 --> 00:02:04,810
The check digit, that last nine, was

49
00:02:04,810 --> 00:02:06,330
chosen to make that happen.

50
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:09,520
So how is that last digit chosen?

51
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:11,760
Leave a space for the check digit, then

52
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:13,600
work from right to left, starting by

53
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,920
doubling the digit next to that space.

54
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:17,990
Here the first seven digits add up to

55
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,160
forty one. The next number that ends in

56
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:21,520
zero is fifty.

57
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:24,160
Fifty minus forty one is nine, so the

58
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:25,840
check digit is nine.

59
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:27,920
If the total already ended in zero, the

60
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:29,600
check digit would be zero.

61
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,360
Change any one of the other digits, and

62
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:32,960
you'd get a different check digit.

63
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:35,360
That's how a Luhn check digit is chosen.

64
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:36,880
Now make a typo.

65
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:39,120
Say you type a seven where the second four

66
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:39,700
should be. Four five three nine, one seven

67
00:02:39,710 --> 00:02:43,600
eight nine. That

68
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,920
digit wasn't doubled, so the total goes up

69
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:46,790
by three, to fifty three.

70
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,680
Fifty three doesn't end in zero.

71
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:51,760
The number fails the check, and the form

72
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:53,520
can flag it right there.

73
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:55,920
Here's why this never misses a typo that

74
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:57,760
replaces one digit with another.

75
00:02:57,760 --> 00:02:59,440
For the digits that aren't doubled,

76
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:01,920
changing one changes the total by one to

77
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:04,000
nine. That can never land on another

78
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,360
number ending in zero.

79
00:03:05,710 --> 00:03:08,030
For doubled digits, look at what each one

80
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:10,910
turns into. Zero through nine become zero,

81
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:13,870
two, four, six, eight, one, three, five,

82
00:03:13,950 --> 00:03:16,190
seven, nine. All ten results are

83
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:18,910
different. So changing any one digit

84
00:03:18,910 --> 00:03:20,830
always changes the total by something that

85
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:22,670
isn't a multiple of ten.

86
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:24,910
Another common typing mistake is swapping

87
00:03:24,910 --> 00:03:25,790
two neighbors.

88
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:28,510
Type three nine as nine three, and a plain

89
00:03:28,510 --> 00:03:30,270
sum wouldn't notice, because the same

90
00:03:30,270 --> 00:03:31,790
digits are still there.

91
00:03:31,790 --> 00:03:33,950
But one of the pair is doubled and the

92
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:36,430
other isn't. Three doubled plus nine,

93
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:38,510
which is fifteen, becomes nine doubled

94
00:03:38,670 --> 00:03:40,430
plus three, which is twelve.

95
00:03:40,430 --> 00:03:42,510
The total drops to forty seven.

96
00:03:42,510 --> 00:03:43,310
Caught.

97
00:03:43,550 --> 00:03:44,910
It isn't perfect.

98
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:47,310
Swap a zero and a nine that sit side by

99
00:03:47,310 --> 00:03:48,910
side, and the total doesn't change,

100
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:51,310
because zero doubled is zero and nine

101
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:53,070
doubled folds back to nine.

102
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:54,830
That's the only swap of two different

103
00:03:54,830 --> 00:03:56,190
neighbors it misses.

104
00:03:56,190 --> 00:03:58,590
Two wrong digits can sometimes cancel each

105
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:00,750
other out. And passing the check only

106
00:04:00,750 --> 00:04:02,430
tells you the arithmetic works.

107
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:03,950
It can't tell you whether the card was

108
00:04:03,950 --> 00:04:06,430
issued, who owns it, or whether a payment

109
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:07,390
will be approved.

110
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:09,980
Once you know the trick, you'll find check

111
00:04:09,980 --> 00:04:11,340
digits everywhere.

112
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:13,980
The last digit in a standard UPC or EAN

113
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:15,980
barcode is a check digit.

114
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:18,060
It uses alternating weights of one and

115
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:21,020
three. A book's thirteen digit ISBN uses

116
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:22,060
those same weights.

117
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:24,540
And the fifteen digit IMEI you see on your

118
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:26,700
phone ends in a Luhn check digit.

119
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:28,620
The details differ, but the idea is the

120
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:31,660
same: one digit computed from the rest.

121
00:04:31,660 --> 00:04:33,980
This is a big idea in engineering.

122
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:35,980
Add a little redundancy, one digit

123
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:37,580
computed from the others, and you can

124
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:39,420
detect errors without knowing the right

125
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:41,420
answer. For any given set of starting

126
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:43,740
digits, only one of the ten possible last

127
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:45,420
digits passes the check.

128
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:47,900
The same idea, scaled up, helps detect

129
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:50,060
errors in files you download and messages

130
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:52,140
your phone sends, using checksums that are

131
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:53,580
far more powerful.

132
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:55,980
Engineers call this error detection.

133
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:57,340
It doesn't fix the mistake.

134
00:04:57,340 --> 00:04:59,020
It just tells you something is wrong, so

135
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:00,540
you can type it again.

136
00:05:00,540 --> 00:05:03,260
Your turn. What check digit makes one two

137
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:05,260
three four pass the check?

138
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:06,620
Pause and try it.

139
00:05:07,090 --> 00:05:08,210
Start from the right.

140
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:09,970
The check digit goes at the end, so the

141
00:05:09,970 --> 00:05:12,050
four next to it gets doubled, to eight.

142
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:13,010
Keep the three.

143
00:05:13,010 --> 00:05:14,690
Double the two, to four.

144
00:05:14,690 --> 00:05:15,730
Keep the one.

145
00:05:15,730 --> 00:05:18,370
Eight plus three plus four plus one is

146
00:05:18,370 --> 00:05:20,770
sixteen. The next number ending in zero is

147
00:05:20,770 --> 00:05:22,930
twenty, so the check digit is four.

148
00:05:22,930 --> 00:05:25,090
One two three four four.

149
00:05:25,410 --> 00:05:28,050
So the next time a checkout form flags a

150
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:29,890
completed card number before you submit

151
00:05:29,890 --> 00:05:32,210
it, this little calculation may be the

152
00:05:32,210 --> 00:05:34,930
reason. It doubles every second digit,

153
00:05:34,930 --> 00:05:37,330
adds everything up, and checks for a zero

154
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:40,370
at the end. One extra digit catches every

155
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:42,930
one digit substitution, and all but one

156
00:05:42,930 --> 00:05:45,010
kind of swap between different neighboring

157
00:05:45,010 --> 00:05:47,490
digits. It's a tiny piece of math hiding

158
00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:48,290
in your wallet.

159
00:05:48,290 --> 00:05:50,290
If you enjoyed this one, please give it a

160
00:05:50,290 --> 00:05:52,130
like and subscribe for more everyday

161
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:53,650
engineering. Thanks for watching!
